    ('lego-vintage-set-of-the-week-1974s-bathroom-265-1', '1a9b7368eba86f8e44423aee875d6f7d', $b455$Brickset is dusting off another piece of LEGO history with their latest Vintage Set of the Week: the 1974 Bathroom (265-1). Back when Homemaker sets were a thing, LEGO decided to give us a peek into the daily ablutions of the 70s. This set, released in 1974, boasts 147 pieces and was part of the larger Homemaker series. It's the kind of set that sparks conversations, mostly about how far LEGO has come in terms of design and functionality.

The comments section on Brickset is a treasure trove of fan reactions, and this set is no exception. Users are reminiscing about owning it, noting its age as the "oldest set in my collection." Others are humorously pointing out the design choices. The toilet, for one, is described as looking "uncomfortable," and the lid's posing has led to some very specific, and slightly alarming, scenarios. One commenter even jokes about needing a "chrome tap battle pack," which, frankly, sounds like a great idea.

There's also a robust discussion around the "grooveless tiles" and the infamous "STAMP" sticker on the bathtub. For those unfamiliar, STAMPs (Stickers Applied to a Manufactured Product) were a LEGO innovation that caused considerable debate back in the day. The idea was to have stickers that were more integrated, but as one user points out, they can be a nightmare to disassemble, essentially turning parts into unbuildable elements. This particular sticker, featuring a fish, is already noted as potentially peeling, adding to the set’s charmingly dated appeal.

The lack of modern conveniences is also a point of contention. No shower, no toilet paper, and a lid that seems to be more of a suggestion than a functional part. It seems the 1974 bathroom experience, at least in brick form, was a bit more... Basic. Compared to today's detailed minifigure accessories, this set is a stark reminder of LEGO's early days.

