    ('microscale-moonshot-a-stellar-creation-from-marc-linares', 'c150865f33c16dc641f60429f475ec76', $b455$This microscale creation by Marc Linares, also known as Syrdarian, is what happens when a builder stretches their limits. Linares, a RebelLUG member, consistently participates in competitions and collaborations, pushing his techniques and learning from others. His latest build, "Microscale Moonshot," is a striking scene depicting extra-solar exploration on the planet Gothea. Imagine miners working in domed settlements beneath a frozen moon. It’s a compact world, but packed with detail.

The banded horizon here brings to mind the popular Tales of the Space Age LEGO Ideas set. The rockets and domes echo the colour palette of the LEGO City Space theme. But what truly stands out is the sheer texture and depth Linares has achieved in such a small space. The icy moon isn't just blue; it's a gradient of blues with incredibly clever textural elements that give it a realistic, chilling appearance. The planetary vista itself is a masterclass in layering, using a mix of studs-up and SNOT (Studs Not On Top) techniques. Some areas are built just a brick tick higher, others are smoothed with tiles, creating multiple layers that draw you into the scene.

Look closer, and the incredible details come into focus. There's a hill cleverly constructed from a minifigure turkey piece, a small touch that adds a lot of character. This isn't just a static display; it's a tiny world brimming with narrative potential. Linares has a knack for making microscale feel vast and lived-in.

For fans of Linares' microscale work, there's another opportunity to get involved. His "Village in the Sky" model is up for a vote in the Bricklink Designer Program Series 10. This particular project even includes kinetic functions, a collaboration with Leewan, another talented designer. Voting is open until February 20th, so if you’re keen to see more of Linares’ innovative designs become official sets, now’s the time to cast your ballot. The energy and creativity poured into this microscale moonshot are undeniable, proving that sometimes, the smallest builds can have the biggest impact.

