    ('bricklink-designer-program-gets-a-makeover-in-2028', '7b6e26e2b23b0ff01e62ffaa0834f4ca', $b455$LEGO fans, brace yourselves. While the plastic brick giant often makes headlines with massive UCS sets or surprise movie tie-ins, the real excitement for many has been bubbling up from the BrickLink Designer Program. This initiative, which allows fan-designed MOCs to become official LEGO sets, is getting a significant internal refresh starting in 2028. Don't expect new sets to suddenly appear, though; this is all about how they'll be organized and presented.

The biggest change? No more "Series." From the 2028 production year onwards, BrickLink will refer to its release cycles as "Waves." So, what would have been Series 12 is now 2028 Wave 1. This is intended to make shipping timelines clearer at a glance, with each year potentially hosting up to three distinct waves. Existing Series 9, 10, and 11 sets will retain their original naming conventions, so no need to update your collection’s metadata.

Beyond the naming, the BrickLink Designer Program icon is also getting a refresh. It will remain bright light orange but will adopt a simpler, more scalable 1x1 LEGO tile design, aligning with BrickLink's core brand aesthetic. The new packaging will also feature different accent colors to denote each wave: purple for Wave 1, green for Wave 2, and red for Wave 3. The overall box color will be blue, reflecting the main BrickLink brand. These changes aim to provide a more colorful and user-friendly experience for fans tracking the program's releases.

The core of the BrickLink Designer Program remains unchanged. It's still about bringing fan-designed creations to life, and this rebranding aims to streamline the process and presentation rather than altering the fundamental offering. While some might find the changes minor, the intention is to improve clarity and consistency as the program continues to grow and offer unique building experiences that often fill gaps left by LEGO's own mainstream releases.

