    ('buck-rogers-thunderfighter-fan-build-lands-online-will-lego-', '9f65fbebfc96383ab39e6cd987010f34', $b455$Your wallet can relax. For now. A fan has built an incredible UCS-scale Thunderfighter from Buck Rogers in the 25th Century, and it’s not an official LEGO set you can buy. This means you don’t have to immediately check your bank balance for a potential crisis, but it does mean you can’t have it. And that’s a different kind of pain.

The original show, which ran from 1979 to 1981, might not be a household name like Star Wars or Battlestar Galactica, but its design DNA is all over those classics. This Thunderfighter was originally designed by Ralph McQuarrie, the same visionary behind iconic Star Wars ships and characters. He also worked on Battlestar Galactica, and this ship was actually a rejected design for the Colonial Viper. So, if it looks familiar, that’s probably why.

This fan build, shared on The Brothers Brick, captures that retrofuturistic vibe perfectly. The builder, Lino Martins, highlights the sleek profile and the distinctive "greenhouse" canopy. But the real challenge, the part that apparently "killed quite a few brain cells," was getting those sunset stripes just right. It’s a detail that apparently separates the casual observer from a true Buck Rogers fan.

Martins details the build process, including the challenges of integrating various sections and ensuring structural integrity for such a large model. He admits his building process isn't the neatest, with his LEGO room resembling a basement workshop during a chaotic build. The stand needed significant reinforcement to support the weight of the finished Thunderfighter, which was largely dictated by the cockpit and seats. He even cannibalized a LEGO N-1 Starfighter set for inspiration on some of the trickier details.

The creator’s dedication to authenticity, especially with those sunset stripes, is clear. The solution involved a solid round column strengthened by a Technic axle, using octagonal bar pieces to achieve the effect. It’s proof of what dedicated fans can create when official LEGO sets don’t exist.

