    ('lego-76331-batman-v-superman-batmobile-worth-2999', '5cb276d71d3301fdae9f81d876c3fb4e', $b455$Your wallet just breathed a sigh of relief; the LEGO [76331](/sets/76331-batman-v-superman-batmobile) Batman v Superman Batmobile is priced at ₹2,999. At that tag you can still afford a weekend binge of your favourite series without selling your car. I’m not the biggest superhero fan, but I do love a good brick‑build, and this mini‑wave of Batmobiles delivers on both fronts.

The set arrives in a sleek black box that looks like a midnight garage door. Open it and you’re greeted by a heap of dark grey and matte black pieces that scream “stealth mode”. The build itself is surprisingly quick – about an hour for a seasoned builder – because the designer has kept the instruction flow tight and the part count modest. You’ll spend most of that time snapping together the iconic dual‑engine front, the exaggerated rear wing, and the glossy windows that hide the Bat‑tech inside.

Design-wise the 76331 Batmobile leans into the cinematic aesthetic of the 2016 “Batman v Superman” film. The proportions are a bit exaggerated – the front bumper looks like a weaponized grill, and the rear spoiler could double as a launch pad for a Bat‑drone. The set includes a few neat accessories: a removable hood, a detachable gun turret, and a tiny figurine of Batman himself, complete with cape that flutters (well, technically it’s a static pose, but you get the idea). The color palette is limited to blacks, greys, and a splash of yellow for the bat emblem, which keeps the build from looking like a rainbow parade.

Playability is where the set shines for a price this low. The Batmobile can roll on its small wheels, and the front engine can swivel for a bit of drama. The gun turret can be aimed at the figurine or any other DC minifig on your shelf. It’s not a massive open‑world vehicle, but it’s enough to spark a quick chase scene on the coffee table. The build also includes a small brick‑built street lamp that can serve as a backdrop for a Gotham‑night tableau.

Now let’s talk value. At ₹2,999 the set sits comfortably in the “affordable” bracket for most Indian LEGO fans. Compare that to the usual ₹5,000‑₹7,000 range for comparable DC builds, and you’re getting a decent amount of brick‑joy for the price. The parts are mostly re‑usable, and the design language will feel familiar if you already own any of the other two Batmobiles (76332 and 76333). Those sibling sets share many of the same panels, so you can mix‑and‑match parts to create a custom Bat‑fleet without breaking the bank.

One minor gripe is the lack of a more detailed interior. The cockpit is a simple brick‑built cockpit with a single steering wheel and a few control panels. If you’re looking for a highly detailed driver’s seat, you’ll be disappointed. But given the price point, the compromise feels justified. The set also doesn’t include any of the newer micro‑figures that LEGO has been pushing lately, which might make it feel a little dated for collectors chasing the latest releases.

Overall, the LEGO 76331 Batman v Superman Batmobile punches above its weight. It’s a solid build, looks the part, and offers enough playability — but at exactly ₹2,999, you’re paying the international MRP with zero import premium. That’s fair, not a discount, so there’s no rush. For newcomers, it’s a low‑risk entry into the DC universe that still feels heroic.

