    ('lego-fast-furious-dodge-charger-rt-42231-worth-15999', 'd0f32ad59dea809e0e0401987afad12e', $b455$Fifteen thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine rupees. Let that number sit for a second before we talk about the LEGO Fast & Furious Dodge Charger R/T. That’s a lot of plastic for a car that doesn’t even have working doors.

LEGO has finally dropped the Fast & Furious Dodge Charger R/T ([42231](/sets/42231-fast-and-furious-dodge-charger-rt-car)), a set that’s been rumoured for a while. As a fan of the franchise, I was hyped. As a fan of my bank balance, I’m… less so. This isn’t a cheap set by any stretch. It’s a big, detailed model, sure, but the price point is definitely going to make some people pause.

Let’s talk about the build. It’s a Technic build, which means you’re getting a lot of intricate internal mechanisms. This isn't a simple snap-together affair. The Charger R/T is known for its powerful V8 engine, and LEGO has tried to replicate that here. You’ll spend a good chunk of time assembling the engine, the moving pistons, the driveshaft, and all the other mechanical bits that make this car tick. It’s satisfying, in a complex, sometimes frustrating, way. If you enjoy Technic and the engineering side of LEGO, you’ll probably get a kick out of this.

The exterior is where things get interesting. The iconic curves of the 1970 Dodge Charger R/T are mostly captured well. The aggressive front end, the wide stance – it looks the part. LEGO has included plenty of stickers to add those signature details, which is a double-edged sword. They add authenticity, but applying them perfectly can be a headache, and the overall look can sometimes feel a bit too reliant on them. The colour scheme, that deep black with subtle grey accents, is classic.

However, the lack of opening doors is a real miss for a car model at this price. For ₹15,999, I’d expect to be able to open the doors, maybe even the hood and trunk. It feels like a missed opportunity to add playability and display value. It’s a static display piece, and while it’s a good one, it could have been great.

The size is substantial. It’s a hefty model once completed, sitting proudly on a display stand. It’s definitely a conversation starter. But is it enough to justify the price? That’s the million-dollar question, or rather, the ₹15,999 question. Compared to other large-scale vehicle sets, it feels a bit light on features for the money. You’re paying a premium for the Fast & Furious license and the detail in the Technic engine.

