    ('lego-lancer-rpg-mechs-more-brick-built-battle-robots-incomin', '366781b044c3933dab5bb9924b518b19', $b455$Your wallet is probably safe for now. LEGO hasn't announced any official sets based on the Lancer RPG, but a talented builder named Dane Erland is back with a third wave of incredible mechs. If you’re into tabletop gaming and plastic bricks, this is the kind of crossover that makes you wish LEGO listened more to its fans.

The Lancer RPG is known for its diverse factions and unique mech designs. Think military hardliners, bio-technicians, and even reality-warping hackers. Erland’s latest creations showcase this variety beautifully. We’re seeing mechs like the Harrison Armory Saladin, a Defender class mech that uses Hardlight tech. Erland even supplements the brick-built frame with black cloth for a more authentic look, and the rifle and dagger details are top-notch.

Then there’s the HORUS Manticore, a terrifying shock trooper that looks more like a beast than a robot. Erland nails that creepy, menacing posture, fitting for a mech that reportedly recites from the Egyptian Book of the Dead. For fans of control-type units, the Smith-Shimano Corpro Black Witch is another standout, a controller mech that manipulates others on the battlefield. Again, Erland uses cloth effectively to add texture and detail.

The collection also features a smaller, black-ops variant of a previous mech: the HORUS Hecatoncheires. Its trans-pink energy bolts really sell the idea of nanite swarms flaring out like mystical fire. Finally, the HORUS Goblin, an early HORUS model, is recreated with a great use of red wands to capture its prickly, parasitic appearance. These aren't just display pieces; they're detailed enough to double as miniatures for playing the game.

While these are fan builds and not official LEGO products, they show what’s possible. If LEGO were to ever officially license Lancer mechs, Erland’s work sets a high bar. We can only dream of official sets with this level of detail and thematic accuracy.

No official Indian pricing or availability details exist for these fan-built models, as they are not official LEGO sets. However, if LEGO were to produce official Lancer mechs, expect them to be priced similarly to other large, licensed mechs, potentially costing thousands of rupees. That's about the same as 50 large jars of pickle. Keep an eye on MyBrickHouse and Toycra for any official LEGO mechs that might hit the Indian market. Use code ABHINAV12 for 12% off on orders above ₹500 at Toycra.

